{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correction to one line in the verification comment above, and a note on what happens next.\n\n**The `fsutil.py` finding IS already tracked.** The comment records that `src/rebar/_store/fsutil.py`'s bare `import fcntl` (:82, with `fcntl.flock` at :92 and `fcntl.LOCK_UN` at :98) accounted for ~1,395 `No module named 'fcntl'` hits in the Windows sweep, and says \"no ticket I could find\". There is one: **`hyperbolic-humanoid-owl`**, open, p2, titled \"`_store/fsutil.sibling_exclusive_lock` does a function-local `import fcntl` — ModuleNotFoundError on Windows, on the HLC stamp path\". It names those exact lines and adds something the sweep could not show — that this sits on a STORE-WRITE path, since `_store/hlc.py:94` takes that lock around `.rebar/hlc.state`, the hybrid-logical-clock stamp every ticket event needs.\n\nWorth noting WHY the search missed it, since the same miss will recur: the ticket's title leads with the symbol and the import mechanism, not with the error string. A search seeded from the observed failure text (`No module named 'fcntl'`) or from the file path alone does not reach it. That ticket also records that `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` deliberately ALLOWS a lazy in-function import — correct for collectability, silent about runtime — which is the same shape of gap this story exists to close.\n\nNothing to do here: it is out of S1's scope and correctly a separate ticket. Recording only so the evidence comment is not left asserting an absence that is not real.\n\n**AC4 remains the only open criterion, and it is now being worked.** The verification correctly declined to tick it: the two `test_native_*` tests appear nowhere in run `33956014400`'s log, and under `pytest -q` a pass prints nothing, so their absence is CONSISTENT WITH passing but is not the positive empirical proof the criterion demands. Inferring a pass from silence is exactly what AC4 was written to forbid.\n\nThe blocker is that this story's own test module is 7/12 red on real Windows — a test-harness portability defect in S1's deliverable, not a production defect. Five ERRORs come from `_windows_shaped_msvcrt()` doing `import fcntl as host`, which is the POSIX-host simulation and cannot build where `fcntl` does not exist; two `test_no_leg_*` FAILUREs come from the test hiding only `fcntl` while `msvcrt` stays importable, so the selector correctly finds a leg and the test's premise is false there.\n\nThat second one is worth stating plainly because it reads as a failure and is not: **it is positive evidence the production Windows leg works.** The real `acquire()` on `windows-latest` took the real `msvcrt` leg. The test asserts an impossible precondition on that platform, not a broken behaviour.\n\nA fix is in flight — platform-correct skips for the simulation tests and a Windows-aware premise for the `no_leg` pair, changing the tests and not the production lock code, with POSIX behaviour held identical — after which a native result becomes readable by name rather than by absence."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788603573215276402,"uuid":"0209aad9-f16e-4f5b-86bc-83b3f38b85c1"}